WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.464
- The Alicia Keys to success.

00:00:02.464 --> 00:00:03.297
(Alicia trumpeting)

00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:04.130
I need some music.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.690
(laughing)

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.105
I need some music for that.

00:00:07.105 --> 00:00:09.630
People always told me don't
you, you should be wearing

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.985
dresses and be, and it's
just, wasn't really,

00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:13.956
wasn't ever my thing.

00:00:13.956 --> 00:00:17.311
As long as people are like, I feel you,

00:00:17.311 --> 00:00:19.116
that to me is the most important thing.

00:00:19.116 --> 00:00:20.672
I do believe that you can have it all.

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:22.108
You can have everything that you want.

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:25.755
- She's an American singer,
a songwriter, pianist,

00:00:25.755 --> 00:00:28.615
actress, record producer
and philanthropist.

00:00:28.615 --> 00:00:30.533
♫ I keep on fallin'

00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.283
♫ In

00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:39.583
♫ And out of love

00:00:41.378 --> 00:00:43.936
♫ With you

00:00:43.936 --> 00:00:46.210
- Throughout her career,
she's won 15 Grammy awards

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.003
and has sold over 35
million albums worldwide.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:52.132
♫ They could say what they like

00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:56.299
♫ But all I know is everything's
going to be all right

00:00:57.602 --> 00:01:00.418
- Billboard Magazine named
her the top R and B artist

00:01:00.418 --> 00:01:01.772
of the 2000s.

00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:05.022
♫ This girl is on fire

00:01:06.573 --> 00:01:09.823
♫ This girl is on fire

00:01:11.824 --> 00:01:14.193
- She's Alicia Keys, and
here's my take on her top 10

00:01:14.193 --> 00:01:15.628
rules for success.

00:01:15.628 --> 00:01:17.539
Rule number 10 is my personal
favorite, and make sure

00:01:17.539 --> 00:01:19.710
to stick around all the way
to the end for some special

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.902
bonus clips.

00:01:20.902 --> 00:01:22.591
And as always, if Alicia
says something that really

00:01:22.591 --> 00:01:25.268
resonates with you, please
leave it in the comments below

00:01:25.268 --> 00:01:27.814
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:01:27.814 --> 00:01:28.847
as well.

00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:30.048
Enjoy.

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:32.432
(object thudding)

00:01:32.432 --> 00:01:35.515
(invigorating music)

00:01:42.423 --> 00:01:45.840
- So what are the Alicia Keys to success?

00:01:46.892 --> 00:01:49.309
- The Alicia Keys to success.

00:01:50.246 --> 00:01:51.079
(Alicia trumpeting)

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:51.914
I need some music.

00:01:51.914 --> 00:01:53.436
(laughing)

00:01:53.436 --> 00:01:55.765
I need some music for that.

00:01:55.765 --> 00:01:57.199
I don't know, I'm learning as I got, too.

00:01:57.199 --> 00:02:00.226
That's the truth and I think that

00:02:00.226 --> 00:02:03.404
one of the things I've learned
most recently is just really

00:02:03.404 --> 00:02:05.654
to really find who you are.

00:02:06.795 --> 00:02:08.881
Find who you are and don't be so worried

00:02:08.881 --> 00:02:12.348
about who everybody else around you is.

00:02:12.348 --> 00:02:15.409
What people tell you you
should be like or act like

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:17.108
or think like or talk like.

00:02:17.108 --> 00:02:19.410
Like really try to find
it out for yourself

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.640
and figure out what
really resonates with you

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.206
and what you feel, 'cause you know it.

00:02:23.206 --> 00:02:25.763
When you know something's
right for you, you feel it.

00:02:25.763 --> 00:02:27.836
And so, I think a lot of
times, we talk ourselves

00:02:27.836 --> 00:02:29.584
out of it, and we say,
oh, but I don't know,

00:02:29.584 --> 00:02:32.999
my friends feel different, or
my boyfriend feels different,

00:02:32.999 --> 00:02:34.456
or my mother says this.

00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:37.444
And so, we don't get a chance
to really explore who we are.

00:02:37.444 --> 00:02:38.797
So that's one of the things.

00:02:38.797 --> 00:02:41.796
That's one of my most recently
learned keys to success is

00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:44.235
really understanding
who are and embrace it

00:02:44.235 --> 00:02:45.814
and be (mumbling).

00:02:45.814 --> 00:02:50.116
♫ I keep on fallin' in and out

00:02:50.116 --> 00:02:53.199
♫ Of love with you

00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:57.278
♫ I never loved someone

00:02:57.278 --> 00:03:00.904
♫ The way that I'm lovin' you

00:03:00.904 --> 00:03:03.102
♫ Oh baby, uh, uh

00:03:03.102 --> 00:03:06.157
- When I sing that song,
every night, I'm like, wow.

00:03:06.157 --> 00:03:10.487
Who'da thought that all of
this from that, you know

00:03:10.487 --> 00:03:13.727
what I mean, and people told
us that was a bad record

00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:17.810
to go with, nobody plays slow
records, it'll never cross

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.955
over, nobody's going to
get it, you won't make it,

00:03:19.955 --> 00:03:22.195
da-da-da, they told us
everything under the sun.

00:03:22.195 --> 00:03:25.427
But it felt good, and it felt
special, and when people heard

00:03:25.427 --> 00:03:29.703
it they were moved, and all
the pieces just fell into place

00:03:29.703 --> 00:03:32.738
so, it's definitely my calling card.

00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:37.056
It's my little baby, and
I cherish and respect it.

00:03:37.056 --> 00:03:38.806
I love this song, Girl On Fire.

00:03:38.806 --> 00:03:41.296
They all mean something, and
they all came from something

00:03:41.296 --> 00:03:44.213
but Girl On Fire is definitely ill.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.253
It came from this interview that did.

00:03:48.253 --> 00:03:51.391
I was reading it, and
she said something like,

00:03:51.391 --> 00:03:55.224
she's like a girl on
fire, and I was like, uh.

00:03:56.074 --> 00:03:57.157
I loved that.

00:03:58.646 --> 00:04:00.423
We were in the studio one
day, and I was workin'

00:04:00.423 --> 00:04:02.537
with Jeff Bhasker and Salaam Remi.

00:04:02.537 --> 00:04:04.279
I was like, I've been wantin'
to write this song called

00:04:04.279 --> 00:04:05.547
Girl On Fire.

00:04:05.547 --> 00:04:08.167
I'm like, what does
Girl On Fire sound like?

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:11.630
Somehow, we start tossin'
around these ideas and thoughts

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.432
and these different sounds
come out, and these different

00:04:14.432 --> 00:04:15.265
things come out.

00:04:15.265 --> 00:04:18.908
Eventually, we came to the
chords that are Girl On Fire.

00:04:18.908 --> 00:04:22.308
And I guess you'd want
me to play the chords.

00:04:22.308 --> 00:04:25.059
So the chords are pretty...

00:04:25.059 --> 00:04:27.809
(Alicia playing)

00:04:34.236 --> 00:04:35.574
That ain't the chord, hold on.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.176
There, there go the chord.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.259
So, it's like

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:41.013
♫ She's just a girl

00:04:41.013 --> 00:04:43.625
♫ And she's on fire

00:04:43.625 --> 00:04:45.261
So anyway, those are the chords, right?

00:04:45.261 --> 00:04:47.147
So, the CP-70's on it, too.

00:04:47.147 --> 00:04:49.808
It's like a different kind
of a little bit twangier

00:04:49.808 --> 00:04:51.154
type of sound.

00:04:51.154 --> 00:04:54.279
♫ Hotter than a fantasy

00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:55.508
There go the damn chord again.

00:04:55.508 --> 00:04:58.088
♫ Lonely like a highway

00:04:58.088 --> 00:05:00.341
We start goin' through all
these different crazy drums

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:03.531
and then there was these loud,
obnoxious, just destructive

00:05:03.531 --> 00:05:06.133
drums, I was like, yes,
the Girl On Fire is loud

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:09.577
and obnoxious and destructive,
and free, you know

00:05:09.577 --> 00:05:10.410
what I mean.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.843
So, I was like, that's what
a Girl On Fire sounds like.

00:05:11.843 --> 00:05:13.810
And then, we just went into the room.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.227
I just had a mic set up over here.

00:05:16.227 --> 00:05:18.299
♫ Oh

00:05:18.299 --> 00:05:19.251
And it's just flowin'.

00:05:19.251 --> 00:05:21.743
It's like one of these crazy
moments that everything's

00:05:21.743 --> 00:05:23.501
just flowing, which sometimes happens,

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:24.827
sometimes doesn't happen.

00:05:24.827 --> 00:05:27.128
But this moment,
everything was just magic.

00:05:27.128 --> 00:05:29.885
We sat down on the couch, we
start writing some lyrics down,

00:05:29.885 --> 00:05:30.718
I'm like,

00:05:30.718 --> 00:05:32.216
♫ She's just a girl and she's on fire

00:05:32.216 --> 00:05:33.324
♫ Higher than a fantasy

00:05:33.324 --> 00:05:34.265
♫ Hotter than a fan

00:05:34.265 --> 00:05:35.243
♫ Lonely like a highway

00:05:35.243 --> 00:05:36.834
It's just flowing, boom, boom, boom.

00:05:36.834 --> 00:05:38.756
Then we go in again,
and I just sing it again

00:05:38.756 --> 00:05:40.734
from top to bottom, and I
don't know what happened,

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:45.114
but it was like an explosion
of magic fairy dust

00:05:45.114 --> 00:05:47.936
that came down, and that's
basically what happens sometimes

00:05:47.936 --> 00:05:49.697
with songs, but it's very rare.

00:05:49.697 --> 00:05:51.386
I just remember being in the bathroom

00:05:51.386 --> 00:05:52.581
(Alicia giggling)

00:05:52.581 --> 00:05:55.718
and I'm singing the song, and I'm like

00:05:55.718 --> 00:05:57.502
♫ Ah

00:05:57.502 --> 00:05:59.338
and it's just going around
and around and round

00:05:59.338 --> 00:06:00.494
and round and round and round in my head.

00:06:00.494 --> 00:06:02.298
When I left the bathroom,
my husband called me

00:06:02.298 --> 00:06:03.881
and I was like, yo!

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:05.705
And he was like, what's up?

00:06:05.705 --> 00:06:09.622
I said, I think we just
came up with somethin'.

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:16.779
Mmm.

00:06:16.779 --> 00:06:20.946
♫ She's just a girl and she's on fire

00:06:22.441 --> 00:06:25.363
♫ Hotter than a fantasy

00:06:25.363 --> 00:06:27.913
♫ Lonely like a highway

00:06:27.913 --> 00:06:32.080
♫ She's livin' in a world and it's on fire

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:36.216
♫ Filled with catastrophe

00:06:36.216 --> 00:06:38.887
♫ But she knows she can fly away

00:06:38.887 --> 00:06:40.637
♫ Oh

00:06:42.052 --> 00:06:45.404
♫ She got both feet on the ground

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:48.450
♫ And she's burnin' it down

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.990
♫ Oh

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.425
♫ Oh

00:06:53.425 --> 00:06:56.933
♫ Got her head in the clouds

00:06:56.933 --> 00:07:00.683
♫ And she's not backin' down

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:04.935
Now, what I'm going to ask
myself is why did I chose

00:07:04.935 --> 00:07:07.236
to write this song so high.

00:07:07.236 --> 00:07:09.947
That's probably what's going to come up

00:07:09.947 --> 00:07:13.312
on some occasions,
because I didn't even get

00:07:13.312 --> 00:07:16.512
to the chorus yet, and I'm askin' myself

00:07:16.512 --> 00:07:19.295
what made me write this song that high.

00:07:19.295 --> 00:07:21.508
So, now you'd have to wait for the chorus,

00:07:21.508 --> 00:07:23.338
'cause we could have a disaster.

00:07:23.338 --> 00:07:24.913
(laughing)

00:07:24.913 --> 00:07:28.725
Even when I was just just
starting, I was different,

00:07:28.725 --> 00:07:32.385
and I looked way different
and I played the piano

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:34.910
and I had the braids, and I
was straight off of the streets

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:35.955
in New York.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:40.188
♫ I keep on fallin' in and out

00:07:40.188 --> 00:07:43.201
♫ Of love with you

00:07:43.201 --> 00:07:45.855
People always told me don't
you, you should be wearing

00:07:45.855 --> 00:07:48.908
dresses and be, and it just
wasn't really, was never

00:07:48.908 --> 00:07:49.741
my thing.

00:07:50.728 --> 00:07:53.271
My thing, I wanted to be
the everyday girl that I was

00:07:53.271 --> 00:07:57.802
and be able to relate to
other women and girls,

00:07:57.802 --> 00:08:01.346
and be able to just be who
I was, 'cause I didn't see

00:08:01.346 --> 00:08:05.589
myself in the music or in
the entertainment world

00:08:05.589 --> 00:08:06.422
or whatever.

00:08:06.422 --> 00:08:09.942
And so, now, I feel like I
have actually really come

00:08:09.942 --> 00:08:13.399
back to that place, and really
recognizing I've always been

00:08:13.399 --> 00:08:16.847
that person, and as you grow,
you try different things

00:08:16.847 --> 00:08:18.929
and you go different
places, but that's where I'm

00:08:18.929 --> 00:08:21.769
the most comfortable, and
that's where I really am able

00:08:21.769 --> 00:08:23.642
to be truly myself.

00:08:23.642 --> 00:08:25.815
My mother instilled a
lot of principles in me

00:08:25.815 --> 00:08:27.272
just by being her.

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:30.467
But one of the things is she
often instilled in me was also

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:33.634
just to see things through to the end.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.115
Don't give up halfway in
the middle just 'cause

00:08:37.115 --> 00:08:39.692
you tired, see it through.

00:08:39.692 --> 00:08:41.881
So, that's definitely
somethin' that helps me get

00:08:41.881 --> 00:08:43.709
through a lot of things.

00:08:43.709 --> 00:08:46.217
A lot of people who are creative,
they often get shut down

00:08:46.217 --> 00:08:49.382
by their parents, or things
like that, saying it's not

00:08:49.382 --> 00:08:52.024
a real job, or it'll
never work, and I'm glad

00:08:52.024 --> 00:08:54.515
that I was encouraged by her like that.

00:08:54.515 --> 00:08:57.698
My goal is to always be
the realest that I can be,

00:08:57.698 --> 00:09:00.071
and bring the best music that I can bring,

00:09:00.071 --> 00:09:01.810
and that's the goal.

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:02.796
That's the most important thing.

00:09:02.796 --> 00:09:06.111
All the other things
are illusions, I think,

00:09:06.111 --> 00:09:10.046
in a lot of ways, but for me,
as long as people can feel it,

00:09:10.046 --> 00:09:13.659
like you said, as long as
people are like I feel you!

00:09:13.659 --> 00:09:15.482
That to me is the most important thing.

00:09:15.482 --> 00:09:18.108
Everything changed when I
went to Egypt, just because

00:09:18.108 --> 00:09:19.928
it was a turning point in my life.

00:09:19.928 --> 00:09:22.869
You have these points in your
life where you really learn

00:09:22.869 --> 00:09:26.398
something important, and
that was a beginning of me,

00:09:26.398 --> 00:09:30.608
one of my big lessons
which was about really

00:09:30.608 --> 00:09:35.037
even creating the life and
the world that you want

00:09:35.037 --> 00:09:36.358
to live in.

00:09:36.358 --> 00:09:40.275
You don't have to
necessarily live in this life

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:43.693
of chaos and unhappiness.

00:09:44.601 --> 00:09:45.865
What is it?

00:09:45.865 --> 00:09:48.546
What life do you want to have and
what life do you want to live?

00:09:48.546 --> 00:09:50.763
For myself, at that time,
that was the question.

00:09:50.763 --> 00:09:53.626
'Cause I was learning how to
juggle my own personal life

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:57.933
and my professional life, and
how to be happy inside of it.

00:09:57.933 --> 00:10:00.268
And so, that was a big
lesson for me at the time.

00:10:00.268 --> 00:10:02.277
- What's the perfect day for you?

00:10:02.277 --> 00:10:04.777
- Wow, the perfect day for me.

00:10:06.016 --> 00:10:07.529
This is my perfect day.

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:08.692
- Do you remember what it was?

00:10:08.692 --> 00:10:11.293
You said a perfect day
would be waking up at three

00:10:11.293 --> 00:10:13.699
in the afternoon and reading a book.

00:10:13.699 --> 00:10:15.009
- Mmm!

00:10:15.009 --> 00:10:16.584
Still sounds perfect.

00:10:16.584 --> 00:10:19.320
- Back then, and you tell
me if I'm right, it did seem

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.066
to be all about the work.

00:10:21.066 --> 00:10:22.403
- Oh, my gosh, you kidding me.

00:10:22.403 --> 00:10:24.422
It was totally all about the work.

00:10:24.422 --> 00:10:26.229
First of all, I grew up with my mother,

00:10:26.229 --> 00:10:30.278
who was my everything,
and a single parent,

00:10:30.278 --> 00:10:34.557
and she had to work behind
off, and so she also instilled

00:10:34.557 --> 00:10:36.951
that in me, because being
a young girl growing up

00:10:36.951 --> 00:10:40.425
in the city, you have to be busy
or you're going to get lost.

00:10:40.425 --> 00:10:42.489
- [Reporter] What's one song
that still has the ability

00:10:42.489 --> 00:10:44.039
to move you when you sing it?

00:10:44.039 --> 00:10:47.178
- Every song has the ability
to move me when I sing it,

00:10:47.178 --> 00:10:49.316
which is something I'm very grateful for,

00:10:49.316 --> 00:10:52.225
'cause the songs that I
write, they're very personal

00:10:52.225 --> 00:10:55.645
and they're very emotional,
and I can understand them.

00:10:55.645 --> 00:10:59.105
I can understand them, even if
the time has passed already.

00:10:59.105 --> 00:11:00.317
I can understand it.

00:11:00.317 --> 00:11:03.703
I have to say, I'll probably
say Fallin' is the song for me.

00:11:03.703 --> 00:11:06.776
Obviously, I've sung it a
lot, but there's a magic

00:11:06.776 --> 00:11:09.215
about that song that is just unbelievable.

00:11:09.215 --> 00:11:11.267
♫ I keep on fallin'

00:11:11.267 --> 00:11:13.017
♫ In

00:11:17.751 --> 00:11:20.501
♫ And out of love

00:11:22.055 --> 00:11:24.883
♫ With you

00:11:24.883 --> 00:11:27.601
And the way that it
signifies the beginning of

00:11:27.601 --> 00:11:32.436
my career and my life, as
I know it in this world.

00:11:32.436 --> 00:11:34.133
It gives me chills every time.

00:11:34.133 --> 00:11:37.067
♫ It's just a brand new kind of me

00:11:37.067 --> 00:11:38.570
- [Reporter] Well, you're
certainly bucking the odds.

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:41.550
They always say you can't
have it all at the same time.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.176
- I do believe that you can have it all.

00:11:43.176 --> 00:11:44.553
You can have everything that you want.

00:11:44.553 --> 00:11:46.786
If you want it and you
want to work for it,

00:11:46.786 --> 00:11:47.916
you can have it.

00:11:47.916 --> 00:11:50.955
♫ Don't be mad

00:11:50.955 --> 00:11:55.122
♫ It's just a brand new time for me

00:11:56.433 --> 00:11:59.183
(crowd cheering)

00:12:03.454 --> 00:12:04.557
- Thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:07.091
I made this video because
a local fan stopped me

00:12:07.091 --> 00:12:09.973
in the store and said, "Hey,
Evan, you got to do a video

00:12:09.973 --> 00:12:12.768
"on Alicia Keys, she's my favorite."

00:12:12.768 --> 00:12:14.642
So, this one is for you, bro.

00:12:14.642 --> 00:12:16.200
Now, if you guys have
a famous entrepreneur

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.189
that you'd like me to
profile next, leave it down

00:12:18.189 --> 00:12:20.159
in the comments below and
I'll see what I can do.

00:12:20.159 --> 00:12:21.906
I'd also love to know, what did Alicia say

00:12:21.906 --> 00:12:23.553
that really meant the most to you.

00:12:23.553 --> 00:12:26.065
What rule had the biggest
impact on you and why?

00:12:26.065 --> 00:12:27.588
Leave it in the comments.

00:12:27.588 --> 00:12:29.234
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:12:29.234 --> 00:12:30.821
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:12:30.821 --> 00:12:31.846
to Nancy Johnson.

00:12:31.846 --> 00:12:33.937
Nancy, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:12:33.937 --> 00:12:35.109
Your One Word.

00:12:35.109 --> 00:12:37.225
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:12:37.225 --> 00:12:38.557
So, thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:38.557 --> 00:12:39.983
I believe in you.

00:12:39.983 --> 00:12:42.182
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:12:42.182 --> 00:12:44.247
and whatever your one word is.

00:12:44.247 --> 00:12:46.850
Much love, I'll see ya soon.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.683
(object thudding)

00:12:52.832 --> 00:12:54.942
- [Narrator] She turned down
her scholarship for a demo deal

00:12:54.942 --> 00:12:56.220
with Columbia Records.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.205
Alicia co-wrote and recorded
a song which featured

00:12:59.205 --> 00:13:01.094
in the blockbuster film Men In Black.

00:13:01.094 --> 00:13:03.566
It was never released as
a single, and her union

00:13:03.566 --> 00:13:05.505
with Columbia soon ended.

00:13:05.505 --> 00:13:09.047
Alicia then met powerful
industry mogul Clive Davis

00:13:09.047 --> 00:13:11.522
who originally signed
her to Arista Records

00:13:11.522 --> 00:13:13.490
and eventually to J Records.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.515
- Me and my manager Jeff
Robinson, we looked at each other

00:13:15.515 --> 00:13:18.104
like okay, so what are we going to do?

00:13:18.104 --> 00:13:21.058
And we believed enough in
my stuff to find somebody

00:13:21.058 --> 00:13:22.607
who also believed in it.

00:13:22.607 --> 00:13:25.493
So, at the time it was
Arista where Clive was,

00:13:25.493 --> 00:13:27.740
and so he brought my stuff into Clive,

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:29.647
and that was the first
time he ever heard it,

00:13:29.647 --> 00:13:31.909
and he loved it, and so
he wanted to meet me,

00:13:31.909 --> 00:13:35.643
and that was how our
first meeting was set up.

00:13:35.643 --> 00:13:38.192
And when all of that went
well, sorry I'm giving you

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:42.552
the three second version
of it, he moved over to J.

00:13:42.552 --> 00:13:46.553
I had the option to go
numerous places at that time,

00:13:46.553 --> 00:13:50.967
but I felt loyalty to
somebody who was loyal to me

00:13:50.967 --> 00:13:54.632
when I needed help the most,
and so we went over there

00:13:54.632 --> 00:13:55.561
with him.

00:13:55.561 --> 00:13:57.094
I just goin' forward light.

00:13:57.094 --> 00:13:59.570
Positivity, good energy.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:04.037
And I think that, really, I
think you have to really protect

00:14:04.037 --> 00:14:07.669
yourself, and you have to
be cautious of your choices

00:14:07.669 --> 00:14:09.022
and stuff like that.

00:14:09.022 --> 00:14:10.237
But we're all human beings.

00:14:10.237 --> 00:14:13.228
We're all human beings
and no one's perfect.

00:14:13.228 --> 00:14:17.075
So, I just try to keep
forward with light and love

00:14:17.075 --> 00:14:19.197
and that's what I focus on.

00:14:19.197 --> 00:14:21.393
I just don't focus on the negative.

00:14:21.393 --> 00:14:22.939
Yeah, but Girl Can't Be Herself.

00:14:22.939 --> 00:14:25.158
I think that's just been
something that's been on my mind

00:14:25.158 --> 00:14:27.718
so much, and that's part of
what this album is all about.

00:14:27.718 --> 00:14:31.212
It really started from this
place of completely takin'

00:14:31.212 --> 00:14:35.383
down any walls, takin'
down any veils, any reason

00:14:35.383 --> 00:14:39.550
to not 1,000% just speak
truthfully to what I was feeling,

00:14:40.634 --> 00:14:43.749
and what I feel is happenin'
around me and to us.

00:14:43.749 --> 00:14:48.147
And so, Girl Can't Be Herself,
I feel like there's such

00:14:48.147 --> 00:14:51.763
a feelin' for us as women,
that there's such a standard,

00:14:51.763 --> 00:14:55.397
there's such a high level
of scrutiny that happens

00:14:55.397 --> 00:14:59.208
to women and to young girls,
and it's really, really heavy.

00:14:59.208 --> 00:15:02.874
It's intense, and it's very
oppressive, and it's too much.

00:15:02.874 --> 00:15:07.411
And I think that from the
minute that we're able to see,

00:15:07.411 --> 00:15:11.507
we're fed this vision of
what a woman looks like,

00:15:11.507 --> 00:15:13.089
or what a woman's supposed
to look like or what

00:15:13.089 --> 00:15:14.472
a sexy woman, or are you beautiful,

00:15:14.472 --> 00:15:15.668
or are you not beautiful.

00:15:15.668 --> 00:15:17.237
It's like whoa.

00:15:17.237 --> 00:15:21.225
So anyway, the deprogramming
that has to happen to all of us

00:15:21.225 --> 00:15:23.661
for many reasons, but
especially for us as women,

00:15:23.661 --> 00:15:25.924
is pretty intense, so I
wanted to write that song

00:15:25.924 --> 00:15:28.734
just because that was how I was feelin'.

00:15:28.734 --> 00:15:31.269
And so, later on, when
it came back around later

00:15:31.269 --> 00:15:33.992
when I was just recognizing
that, I felt like I was in

00:15:33.992 --> 00:15:36.953
a lot of ways just becoming
a bit of a, almost like

00:15:36.953 --> 00:15:40.865
a bit of a slave to always
feeling like I had to be made up

00:15:40.865 --> 00:15:44.731
in order to go pick up my
son from school, go to the

00:15:44.731 --> 00:15:48.174
grocery store, go down the
block to meet my friend.

00:15:48.174 --> 00:15:50.742
And this was my own doing.

00:15:50.742 --> 00:15:54.909
This is something that I
felt that I needed to do.

00:15:57.547 --> 00:15:58.729
I didn't want to feel
like that all the time.

00:15:58.729 --> 00:16:01.032
I felt it made me feel crazy.

00:16:01.032 --> 00:16:04.927
So again, I was expressing
how I felt in my personal

00:16:04.927 --> 00:16:08.005
journey, and it definitely
has never been anything

00:16:08.005 --> 00:16:10.081
about starting a movement.

00:16:10.081 --> 00:16:11.177
Let's all do it!

00:16:11.177 --> 00:16:13.273
It wasn't about that, and
it's definitely not about

00:16:13.273 --> 00:16:15.561
anyone who does wear
makeup is now something,

00:16:15.561 --> 00:16:16.881
it's not about that.

00:16:16.881 --> 00:16:19.684
It's definitely like I want
women to feel empowered

00:16:19.684 --> 00:16:22.215
to do what they feel
is best for themselves.

00:16:22.215 --> 00:16:24.973
Not because your man told
you and your daddy told you

00:16:24.973 --> 00:16:27.063
and your homegirl said, oh
girl, you look like this,

00:16:27.063 --> 00:16:29.696
or because your boss said if you don't.

00:16:29.696 --> 00:16:32.837
It's like what feels beautiful to you?

00:16:32.837 --> 00:16:34.794
So that you can just feel yourself.

00:16:34.794 --> 00:16:36.622
And whatever that is,
whatever expression that is,

00:16:36.622 --> 00:16:39.787
you do that, and do that
fully, and don't hold back,

00:16:39.787 --> 00:16:42.701
and don't let nobody tell
you that you're not beautiful

00:16:42.701 --> 00:16:44.329
in any way that you want to express it.

